Yes, I can help you with that. SWT (Standard Widget Toolkit) is an open-source widget toolkit for Java that provides GUI components for building desktop applications.

To create a simple example, let's create a window with a button that prints "Hello World!" to the console when clicked.

Here's the code:
```java
import org.eclipse.swt.SWT;
import org.eclipse.swt.events.SelectionAdapter;
import org.eclipse.swt.events.SelectionEvent;
import org.eclipse.swt.layout.RowLayout;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Button;
import org.eclipse.swt.widgets.Shell;

public class HelloWorldSWT {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new shell (window)
        Shell shell = new Shell();

        // Set the title of the window
        shell.setText("Hello World SWT");

        // Create a row layout
        shell.setLayout(new RowLayout());

        // Create a button with label "Click me!"
        Button button = new Button(shell, SWT.PUSH);
        button.setText("Click me!");

        // Add a selection listener to the button
        button.addSelectionListener(new SelectionAdapter() {
            @Override
            public void widgetSelected(SelectionEvent e) {
                System.out.println("Hello World!");
            }
        });

        // Open the window
        shell.open();

        // Run the event loop
        while (!shell.isDisposed()) {
            if (!shell.getDisplay().readAndDispatch()) {
                shell.dispose();
            }
        }
    }
}
```
